This extension adds Railroader modding support to Vortex Mod Manager.
[summary]Unity Mod Manager automatically installed.[/summary]

[b]✅ Supported Versions:[/b]
[list]
[*]Steam[/*]
[*]Other versions may need to select the installation location manually.[/*]
[/list]
[b]🛠️ Mod Installation Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*][b][color=#ffff00]Unity Mod Manager is downloaded and installed automatically. Vortex applies the patch for you, so there is no need to open UMM and click Install. You must be logged into Nexus Mods in Vortex for the download to work.[/color][/b][/*]
[*][b][color=#ea9999]NOTE: Railloader's official download site is currently offline, so Vortex cannot fetch it for you. If you already have a Railloader archive, install it through Vortex like any other mod and it will be placed in the game folder.[/color][/b][/*]
[*]Root Folder mods with a "Railroader_Data" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Railloader installs to the game folder, recognised by a "railloader.exe" file.[/*]
[*]Unity Mod Manager installs to the game folder, recognised by a "UnityModManager.exe" file.[/*]
[*]Mods with an "info.json" file install to the "Mods\<ModName>" folder.[/*]
[*]Mods with a "Definition.json" file install to the "Mods\<ModName>" folder.[/*]
[*]Assembly DLL mods with an "Assembly-CSharp.dll" file install to the "Railroader_Data\Managed" folder.[/*]
[*]Assets/Resources File mods with a ".assets" file install to the "Railroader_Data" folder.[/*]
[*]Any other mod not described above installs to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Archives with a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with ModuleConfig.xml) are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📋 Usage Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]Both UMM mods ("info.json" plus a .dll) and Railloader mods ("Definition.json") are handled no matter how the author packed the archive - files at the top level, wrapped in a "Mods" folder, or wrapped in a folder of their own all end up in one correctly named mod folder.[/*]
[*]Railloader mods do not need Railloader to be present in order to install, only to run. You can set up your mod list first.[/*]
[*]You can open several useful files/folders/URLs using the buttons within the folder icon on the Mods toolbar.[/*]
[*]The "Run Unity Mod Manager" button opens UMM's own window, already pointed at Railroader. Its Mods tab and Assembly install type stay available if you want them.[/*]
[*]Unity Mod Manager's in-game overlay opens with Ctrl+F10 as usual.[/*]
[*]Unity Mod Manager is installed as a normal Vortex mod, so purging or removing it takes the patch back out and leaves a clean game folder. There is no need to uninstall it through UMM.[/*]
[*][color=#ffff00][b]Automatically downloads and installs [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/site/mods/21]Unity Mod Manager[/url]. [/b][/color][color=#ffffff]Vortex applies the patch for you, so there is no need to open UMM and click Install.[/color][/*]
[*]Railloader mods (a "Definition.json") are installed to "Mods&#92;&lt;Mod Name&gt;". They do not need Railloader to be present in order to install, only to run.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📝 Notes for Mod Authors:[/b]
[spoiler]
Packaging rules for Railroader mods, so Vortex installs them to the right place.

Vortex decides what a mod is by looking at the files and folders inside the archive. It tries each installer in order and the first one that matches wins, so archive layout is what determines where your mod ends up.

========================================================================

Root / Game Folder Mods:

For mods laid out the same way the files appear inside the game folder. Vortex copies the matched folder and everything under it straight into the game.

[code]
MyRootMod.zip
└── Railroader_Data\
    └── ... files in their real relative locations
[/code]

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "Railroader_Data" or "Railroader_Data" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Zipping the folder that CONTAINS the game folders, instead of the game folders themselves, adds an extra level and misplaces every file.

========================================================================

Railloader (tool):

This installer handles Railloader, not mods for it. It exists so users can install Railloader through Vortex, and mod authors normally never package this.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file named "railloader.exe" in the archive.
    - Railloader has no working download site at the moment, so Vortex cannot fetch it. This installer exists so an archive you already have installs to the right place.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If you bundle Railloader inside your mod archive, Vortex treats the whole download as Railloader rather than as your mod. Ship the mod alone and list Railloader as a requirement.

========================================================================

Unity Mod Manager (tool):

This installer handles Unity Mod Manager, not mods for it. It exists so users can install Unity Mod Manager through Vortex, and mod authors normally never package this.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file named "UnityModManager.exe" in the archive.
    - Vortex reproduces the loader patch the manager would otherwise apply, so it deploys and purges like any other mod.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If you bundle Unity Mod Manager inside your mod archive, Vortex treats the whole download as Unity Mod Manager rather than as your mod. Ship the mod alone and list Unity Mod Manager as a requirement.

========================================================================

Unity Mod Manager Mods:

Mods for Unity Mod Manager, told apart by their manifest. Each one gets its own folder under "Mods".

[code]
MyUmmMod.zip
├── info.json
└── MyUmmMod.dll
[/code]

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file named "info.json" at any level of the archive. A ".dll" is not required.
    - Every folder holding "info.json" is installed as its own mod, so one archive can carry several mods.
    - The mod folder name comes from the folder holding the manifest. A flat archive is named from the "Id" field in "info.json" instead, so keep that field filled in.
    - Folders above a mod folder, such as a "Mods" wrapper, are dropped.

  Installs to: "Mods\<ModName>"

  Common mistakes:
    - Keeping files a mod needs outside its own folder - only the contents of each "info.json" folder are installed.

========================================================================

Railloader Mods:

Mods for Railloader, the game's second mod loader. They live in the same "Mods" folder as Unity Mod Manager mods, and are told apart by their manifest.

[code]
MyRailloaderMod.zip
└── MyRailloaderMod\
    ├── Definition.json
    └── MyRailloaderMod.dll
[/code]

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file named "Definition.json".
    - A leading "Mods" folder in the archive is dropped - the installer already targets "Mods", so it is not doubled up.
    - A flat archive is named from the "id" field in "Definition.json".

  Installs to: "Mods\<ModName>"

  Common mistakes:
    - Mixing the two formats in one archive. A mod needs either "Definition.json" for Railloader or a Unity Mod Manager manifest, not both - each loader rejects the other's mods.

========================================================================

Assembly Replacement Mods:

Mods that replace a compiled game assembly outright. These overwrite core game files, so they conflict with any other mod touching the same assembly.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file named "Assembly-CSharp.dll" or "Assembly-CSharp-firstpass.dll".

  Installs to: "Railroader_Data\Managed"

  Common mistakes:
    - Assembly replacements cannot be combined with other assembly mods - state this clearly on the mod page.
    - Shipping an assembly alongside a plugin makes the whole archive install as an assembly mod.

========================================================================

Asset Replacement Mods:

Mods that replace packed Unity asset files, deployed into the game's data folder.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file with the ".assets", ".resource" or ".ress" extensions.

  Installs to: "Railroader_Data"

  Common mistakes:
    - Asset files must keep their original names to replace the right bundle.

========================================================================

Fallback Installer:

The catch-all. Any archive that matched none of the installers above lands here and is copied across unchanged.

[b][color=#ffff00]NOTE: Landing in the fallback installer is a signal your archive layout needs fixing.[/color][/b]

  Requirements:
    - Reaching this installer usually means the archive was not laid out in a way Vortex recognised.
    - Vortex shows the user a notification when a mod installs through the fallback.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If your mod lands here unintentionally, re-check the layouts above - users will see a fallback warning and may report the mod as broken.

========================================================================

Rules That Apply To Every Mod Type:

    - Archives that contain a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with "ModuleConfig.xml") are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead, and none of the rules above apply.
    - Folder and file name matching is case-insensitive.
    - Extra wrapper folders around a recognised folder are generally fine; the installer searches at any depth.
[/spoiler]
